  date = {2026-04-04T11:33:01Z},
  url = {https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Scale-free_network&oldid=1347050999},
  urldate = {2026-04-17},
  abstract = {A scale-free network is a network whose degree distribution follows a power law, at least asymptotically. That is, the fraction P(k) of nodes in the network having k connections to other nodes goes for large values of k as                        P         (         k         )                   ∼                              k                        −             γ                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle P(k)\textbackslash{} \textbackslash sim \textbackslash{} k\textasciicircum\{\textbackslash boldsymbol \{-\textbackslash gamma \}\}\}    where                         γ                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash gamma \}     is a parameter whose value is typically in the range                         2         {$<$}         γ         {$<$}         3                 \{\textbackslash textstyle 2{$<\backslash$}gamma {$<$}3\}     (wherein the second moment (scale parameter) of                                    k                        −             γ                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle k\textasciicircum\{\textbackslash boldsymbol \{-\textbackslash gamma \}\}\}     is infinite but the first moment is finite), although occasionally it may lie outside these bounds. The name "scale-free" could be explained by the fact that some moments of the degree distribution are not defined, so that the network does not have a characteristic scale or "size". Preferential attachment and the fitness model have been proposed as mechanisms to explain the power law degree distributions in real networks. Alternative models such as super-linear preferential attachment and second-neighbour preferential attachment may appear to generate transient scale-free networks, but the degree distribution deviates from a power law as networks become very large.},
  langid = {english},
